Subject: [PATCH] Add paired flux injection for co-located neutral species

Add the `<species>.flux_pair_with` injector option. Paired species share the
per-cell count, plane location, and weight while retaining independent
momentum distributions, so each created pair is charge-neutral at creation.
Reject ionizable pair members and planes too close to domain boundaries, use the
port's Metal-safe chunked scan, stage both species in temporary containers before
boundary handling, and keep the no-pair path unchanged. Reduced radial geometries
that cannot preserve angular coordinates through the public insertion API are
rejected. Adapted from WarpX 9c054f22..5614f332 to the pinned port base.

diff --git a/Docs/source/usage/parameters.rst b/Docs/source/usage/parameters.rst
index a9a6902db..ed8552c9f 100644
--- a/Docs/source/usage/parameters.rst
+++ b/Docs/source/usage/parameters.rst
@@ -1525,6 +1525,18 @@ Particle initialization
 
       * ``<species_name>.flux_tmax`` (``double``, Optional time at which the flux will be turned off. Ignored when negative.)
 
+      * ``<species_name>.flux_pair_with`` (``string``, optional; name of a second species to inject
+        with shared plane samples. Both species must use ``NFluxPerCell`` with identical
+        plane geometry, bounds, ``num_particles_per_cell``, and flux, and must have equal and
+        opposite nonzero charge. Paired creation at the shared plane uses no partial sub-step push;
+        injection is quantized to time-step boundaries and the created charge cancels locally.
+        The shared plane must be strictly inside the simulation domain and at least one cell
+        away from either domain boundary along its normal direction; planes closer than one
+        cell to a boundary are rejected. Paired species must have fixed charge, so field or
+        impact ionization, per-particle charge-state attributes, and other runtime particle
+        attributes are not supported. Paired
+        injection is not supported in ``RCYLINDER`` or ``RSPHERE`` geometry.)
+
     * ``none``: Do not inject macro-particles (for example, in a simulation that starts with neutral, ionizable atoms, one may want to create the electrons species -- where ionized electrons can be stored later on -- without injecting electron macro-particles).
 
 .. pp:param:: <species_name>.num_particles_per_cell_each_dim
